ettled`, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE HERE.** A verdict row with no ticket at all is in neither set: keying off `live` leaves it flagged exactly as before, while keying off `not settled` would silently excuse it. **THE FALSE POSITIVE WAS LATENT UNTIL HE USED HIS OWN ESCAPE ROUTE, and that is stated plainly rather than dressed up: before the reopen, 0 of 93 TRACKED rows had a ticket in a live-but-not-UNCONFIRMED status. It became realised at 1 of 93, 0.010753, Wilson 95% [0.001901, 0.058419], the moment he reopened 1 of them.** So the repair is justified by the specification mismatch and NOT by a pre-existing error count. **WHY HIS CLOSE WAS WRONG IN THE FIRST PLACE, which the guard was right to catch.** His question has 2 halves — *"So if it is closed, why did it come up again?"* and *"Do we have a 'closed' status in the ticket store that can prevent closed/completed work like this from being endlessly repeated?"* — and only the second was answered and verified. It was closed as though both were. The ticket is now REOPENED and stays REOPENED; B102 is the answer to the first half. **A CLAIM OF MINE WAS REFUTED BY THE FOLLOW STEP BEFORE ANY EDIT WAS MADE, and it is recorded because the near-miss is the argument for the step.** The preceding session stated that a simulated reopen cleared the guard. Executed on a copy of the store, it did not: REOPENED is LIVE and is not UNCONFIRMED, so the guard still refused. Tracing the consumers before touching anything is what caught it; the edit would have been made on a false premise. **GUARD:** `bench/tests/test_a_guard_cannot_refuse_his_own_remedy_2026-11-03.py`, 10 tests, added to the commit gate. Its load-bearing test EXECUTES the real guard against a store differing from the live one in exactly 1 respect — the subject forced back to CLOSED by an appended superseding record, which is how the store itself records a status change — and requires it to raise, naming the subject row. **THAT TEST EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE CAUGHT 2 OF THE 4 MUTATIONS BY SOURCE TEXT ONLY**, which is the weakness `execute-do-not-grep` names. With it, widening `live()` to `all()` and replacing `assert not wrong` with `assert True` both go red by execution. **P-PASS: 6 mutations, 6 red** — reverting to `unconfirmed()`, widening to `all()`, gutting the assertion, pointing the reopen check at a still-CLOSED ticket, and the 2 re-tested by execution. Every restore verified byte-identical by sha256 before continuing. **REVERSIBLE IN 1 LINE if he judges otherwise: put `store.unconfirmed()` back in place of `store.live()` in `bench/tests/test_every_annotation_verdict_names_its_evidence_2026-10-17.py`.**
